<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Kongo dia Ntotila (Kibangu)</h1><p class="st-meta">1666 CE – 1709 CE (approx.) · capital Kibangu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The mountain-based Kongo kingship of Kibangu, one of the rival courts that emerged from Kongo's civil wars and eventually reunited the kingdom in 1709.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kingdom of Kongo's</a> defeat by Portugal at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mbwila">Battle of Mbwila</a> in 1665, the kingdom split among rival royal houses, and the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbanza-kongo">Sao Salvador</a> was abandoned in 1678. One faction made its base on the fortified mountain of Kibangu, east of the old capital, where a line of claimants maintained the title of king of Kongo.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Kibangu kings ruled a small territory but kept the full ceremony and Catholic identity of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo</a> monarchy, hosting Italian Capuchin missionaries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garcia-iii-of-kongo">Garcia III</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-iv-of-kongo">Pedro IV</a> ruled from there. Pedro IV pursued reconciliation among the rival houses, while the prophet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kimpa-vita">Kimpa Vita</a> and her Antonian movement challenged him by reoccupying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbanza-kongo">Sao Salvador</a>; he had her burnt in 1706.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1709 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-iv-of-kongo">Pedro IV</a>'s forces retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbanza-kongo">Sao Salvador</a>, ending the separate Kibangu court and restoring a single, though much weakened, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kingdom of Kongo</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1666 CE – 1709 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central and Southern Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:92.86%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1666 CE – 1709 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kibangu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Garcia III, Pedro IV</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kikongo</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/angola">Angola</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kibangu</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1688&amp;focus=kingdom-of-kongo-dia-ntotila-kibangu" aria-label="Kingdom of Kongo dia Ntotila (Kibangu) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5163 2630 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Kongo dia Ntotila (Kibangu) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5395 2668h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5483 2886h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5483 2886h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Angola. <a href="/atlas?year=1688&amp;focus=kingdom-of-kongo-dia-ntotila-kibangu">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
